1. Design Philosophy
This architecture is a Recurrent Depth Transformer (RDT) — a three-block design (Prelude
→ Recurrent Core → Coda) where a single shared-weight core block is looped R times
during a single forward pass. Reasoning depth scales with loop count, not parameter count.
The key insight from CART experimental data: recurrent depth achieves better perplexity
than feed-forward depth at equal parameter count.
The v1 paper claim: the Toroidal Expert Sheet, used as the FFN replacement inside the
recurrent core of an RDT, beats matched-FLOP alternatives (dense FFN, vanilla top-k MoE)
at equal training token budget.
Core innovations beyond CART:
Toroidal expert sheet (cortical column analog) replacing standard FFN in core
Multi-head Latent Attention (MLA) replacing standard attention in core (KV compression
critical for R-times-looped attention)
Loop Index Embedding (LIE) for loop-depth differentiation
Sliding window attention + learned sink tokens in prelude
Gated residual bypass around the toroidal expert sheet
Linear time-invariant (LTI) recurrent injection of prelude state into each core loop
Explicitly out of scope for v1 (see Section 11): SSM in core, manifold-constrained hyper-
connections, depth-wise LoRA per loop, DSA, long-context (≥16K) training.
