Why experts write full 10×10 including overlap: overlap cells receive writes from two
experts simultaneously (corners receive writes from four experts) — this is the lateral
communication mechanism. Aggregation resolves the multi-expert conflict at each overlap
cell. Without writing to overlap, there is no cross-expert communication — the entire point
of the torus topology is lost.
Why staggered init: identical init risks experts collapsing to the same function (symmetry
problem). Small offset δ=0.001 × expert_index seeds each expert differently, allowing
gradient descent to push them toward distinct subspace specializations.
Cortical column analogy: each expert is a cortical column. The loop dimension is cortical
depth. Same expert weights applied to the same subspace across 16 loops = column
processing input feature through successive abstraction layers. Torus overlap = lateral
horizontal and vertical connections between columns.
Why corner cells receive 4 contributors: at the corner of any expert’s 10×10 patch, four
10×10 patches meet (the expert itself, its right neighbor, its bottom neighbor, and its
bottom-right diagonal neighbor — all subject to torus wrap at boundaries). Each of these
four experts writes to the same corner dim, requiring 4-way aggregation rather than the 2-
way aggregation sufficient for non-corner edge cells.
Why corner overlap matters architecturally: corner cells are the only locations on the
sheet where information from diagonally-adjacent experts mixes directly. Without corner
overlap, expert (i,j) would only communicate laterally with its 4 axis-aligned neighbors (up,
down, left, right). Corner overlap extends this to all 8 surrounding experts, making the torus
topology genuinely 2D rather than effectively two interleaved 1D rings. The 64 corner dims
are small in count but topologically critical — they are the lateral coupling sites that
distinguish a true torus from a rectangular grid with edge connections.
