Why early exit: R=16 is maximum — simple inputs (lookup, extraction) may converge at 4–6
loops. Probe predicts when additional loops won’t meaningfully change output. Reduces
average inference latency without quality loss on easy tasks. Frozen Phase 1 because probe
learns on noisy signal if model itself is unstable — Phase 2 model is stable enough for probe
to learn genuine convergence patterns.
v1 paper note: the probe is trained but evaluation runs report two numbers — quality with
min
_loops=R=16 (full depth, no early exit) and quality with the probe active. This isolates
the probe’s effect from the toroidal sheet’s effect on the headline result.
5.6 LTI Recurrent Injection
A_matrix = diagonal [1024] # 1,024 params
B_matrix = diagonal [1024] # 1,024 params
formula = h_{t+1} = A·h_new + B·e
A_init = 0.9 # near-identity, stable decay
B_init = 0.1 # weak e injection, grows with training
stability = |A| < 1 enforced # spectral radius constraint
e_gradient = stop-gradient # e.detach() — e is constant anchor
application = end of each loop, after early exit probe
Why LTI: without e re-injection, h drifts arbitrarily across 16 loops — loses grounding in
original input. LTI formula h_{t+1} = A·h + B·e is a linear time-invariant system: A controls
how much prior state persists (memory), B controls how strongly e anchors each loop
(grounding). |A|<1 ensures spectral radius constraint — system is stable, no residual
explosion. Diagonal-only A, B keeps param count minimal (2×1024) while giving per-
dimension control. Stop-gradient on e: prevents gradient from flowing back into prelude via
LTI at every loop (which would mean R× prelude backward passes — expensive and
destabilizing).
