Why hybrid Muon + AdamW: Muon orthogonalizes gradient updates via Newton-Schulz
iterations, producing ~20–35% sample efficiency gains over AdamW on LM training.
DeepSeek V4 uses this same hybrid pattern (Muon for hidden weights, AdamW for
embeddings/norms) at trillion-parameter scale; it has been validated for stable convergence
at scale.
Why exclude embeddings, RMSNorm, etc.: Muon is designed for full-rank 2D weight
matrices. Embeddings and unembedding require stable token-space updates (DeepSeek’s
hybrid pattern explicitly excludes these). RMSNorm weights are 1D and Muon’s ndim ≥ 2
requirement excludes them automatically.
Why FLOP overhead is negligible: per Keller Jordan’s empirical analysis, Muon adds <1%
FLOP overhead in typical LM training. The Newton-Schulz iterations are cheap matrix ops
relative to forward/backward pass.
Why Windows compatibility matters: development environment is Windows 11. Standard
Muon (KellerJordan/Muon) is pure PyTorch — works wherever PyTorch+CUDA works. Flash-
Muon’s Triton default does not work reliably on Windows. RTX 3090 is SM 8.6, so
use_syrk=False is required; the code falls back gracefully.
v1 simplicity note: with mHC and depth-wise LoRA excluded, the optimizer landscape is
two regimes (Muon, AdamW) instead of four (Muon, AdamW, Riemannian SGD on Stiefel,
AdamW with LoRA LR multiplier). Convergence debugging is correspondingly simpler.
