Why truncated BPTT K=8: full BPTT (16 loops) stores 16× activations — memory
prohibitive. K=8 halves activation memory while providing sufficient gradient signal for core
weight learning. Early loops (0–7) receive indirect gradient via the LTI chain. v1
simplification note: with depth-wise LoRA removed, K=8 no longer creates the
asymmetric-LoRA-training problem flagged in the RTCC2 Rev 3 critique. The remaining core
weights are shared across all loops, so K=8 trains them on signal from loops 8–15 with no
per-loop credit-assignment hole.
Why stop-grad on e: e re-injected R times — without stop-grad, gradient flows back
through prelude R times per step. Prohibitive compute and destabilizing. Prelude receives
gradient only from its own forward pass output.
