11. Deferred to v2
The following architectural elements were evaluated for inclusion in v1 and deferred. This
section documents what was deferred, why, and the conditions under which each is
reconsidered for v2.
11.1 SSM (Mamba-style) in core
Status: Deferred to v2.
v1 rationale: SSM contribution is unmeasured at this scale. Including it confounds the
toroidal sheet ablation — if RTCC v1 with SSM beats baseline, attribution between SSM and
torus is unclear without an additional ablation slot.
Reconsider when: v1 paper has landed; v2 ablation budget can include “RTCC + SSM” as a
clean comparison against baseline RTCC. Particularly relevant if v2 extends to long context
where SSM’s O(n) accumulation has structural value.
11.2 Manifold-constrained Hyper-Connections (mHC)
Status: Deferred to v2.
v1 rationale: mHC adds Riemannian SGD on a Stiefel manifold to the optimizer mix, raising
the count of optimizer regimes from two to four. At this scale, the optimization-complexity
cost is not justified by measured gain (it is unmeasured). Cross-loop memory in v1 is
provided by LTI injection of e, which is both simpler and load-bearing for stability.
Reconsider when: v2 includes long-context training where cross-loop memory pressure
increases, or when scaling to ≥1B params where mHC’s published gains were observed.
11.3 Depth-wise LoRA per loop per expert
Status: Deferred to v2.
v1 rationale: depth-wise LoRA per loop interacts badly with truncated BPTT K=8 — early-
loop LoRAs (loops 0–7) receive gradient only through the LTI chain and undertrain relative
to late-loop LoRAs (loops 8–15). Either fix (drop early-loop LoRAs, increase K, or drop LoRA
entirely) is a v2 design decision.
Reconsider when: v2 either (a) increases K to cover all loops, (b) uses LoRA only on loops
8–15 by design, or (c) finds that LIE alone provides insufficient loop differentiation in v1
results.
11.4 DeepSeek Sparse Attention (DSA)
Status: Deferred to v2.
v1 rationale: DSA’s published gains are at long context (≥16K) where attention cost
dominates. v1 caps training at 8K, where full MLA is computationally tractable on a 3090
and DSA’s lightning indexer overhead exceeds its savings. Adding DSA to v1 would mean
training the indexer on a model that doesn’t need sparsity — a clean way to introduce a
confounder.
Reconsider when: v2 extends training to ≥16K context. At that scale DSA becomes
structurally necessary on a 3090 and its inclusion is well-motivated. The DeepSeek
V3.1→V3.2 continued-training pattern (warm up indexer on stable checkpoint, then
unfreeze) is the validated approach.
11.5 Engram Conditional Memory
Status: Deferred to v2 or beyond (carried forward from RTCC2 Rev 3 deferral).
v1 rationale: Engram was validated at 27B params; gains may not translate to ~200M.
Prelude FFN at 6× expansion already targets factual breadth aggressively. Including Engram
in v1 would replace rather than add, restructuring work without clear marginal benefit at this
scale.
Reconsider when: scaling beyond 1B params (e.g., the planned 4096-dim full architecture
build), or when training data shifts toward encyclopedic content (>20%
Wikipedia/reference). Recommended placement: in the prelude between input embedding
and SWA layer 1, with the Engram embedding table sized to claim 20–25% of total params
per the Sparsity Allocation Law.
11.6 16K Context (Phase 3)
Status: Deferred to v2.
v1 rationale: entangled with DSA deferral above. Without DSA, full MLA at 16K with R=16
loops is wallclock-prohibitive on a 3090.
Reconsider when: simultaneously with DSA inclusion in v2.
11.7 Coda FFN 6× expansion
Status: Deferred to v2 ablation.
v1 rationale: RTCC2 Rev 3 specified 6× for “output expressivity,” but this was qualitative
and unablated. The leverage argument that justified upsizing the prelude FFN to 6× does
not extend to the coda — prelude e is re-injected R=16 times so factual capacity there
compounds 16-fold; coda runs once at the end with no re-injection. The asymmetric
leverage makes the unablated jump less defensible at the output, so v1 holds coda at 4× as
the conservative default.
Reconsider when: v2 ablation slot available for “RTCC + coda 6×” vs. “RTCC + coda 4×”
comparison. If prelude 6× shows clear benefit in v1 results, that increases the prior on coda
6× being worth testing — but the magnitude is expected to be smaller because of the
missing re-injection multiplier.
11.8 TIE — Token Index Embedding re-injection per recurrent loop
Status: Deferred to v2.
Concept: Re-inject token positional information at every recurrent loop iteration as an
additive signal to the hidden state, in addition to the position information already carried in
via attention’s RoPE. Each loop iteration would receive h_t + TIE(token_position) , giving
the recurrent core depth-aware position sensitivity rather than depth-invariant position
sensitivity. Biological analog: beta-band rhythm — sequence-position modulation of cortical
processing depth.
What it would buy if included: the recurrent-depth architecture’s structural weakness is
that all R loop iterations see identical positional context — position was injected once at the
embedding layer and decays as it propagates. TIE re-injection lets the core learn iteration-
by-position specialization (e.g., loop 12 might attend differently to early-sequence positions
than loop 4). Cost is near zero — small projection of an existing signal — and
implementation risk is very low.
Closest prior art: Universal Transformer (Dehghani et al., ICLR 2019) injects both step
embedding and positional embedding at each step, though framed as a single combined
addition before the block rather than as two separate additive injections into hidden state
mid-block.
v1 rationale: LIE is the only loop-symmetry-breaking mechanism in v1 (depth-wise LoRA
having been excluded). Adding TIE would consume an ablation slot to prove its independent
contribution and would weaken the “minimum architecture for the central claim” framing.
Universal Transformer precedent means TIE is not a novelty contribution; it is a
stability/expressivity addition.
Reconsider when: v1 results show LIE alone provides insufficient loop differentiation (e.g.,
loops 4–15 produce nearly identical hidden states on a probe), or v2 introduces
architectural elements where per-loop position awareness becomes structurally necessary.
11.9 Sliding Window Buffer (Buffer_alpha)
Status: Deferred to v2.
Concept: A fixed-size compressed summary of the last K tokens (e.g., K=7) held frozen
between updates, then refreshed with a new compressed summary every K tokens. The
frozen buffer is injected additively into every recurrent loop iteration, providing a stable
always-present context signal that does not compete with attention for token-level retrieval.
Biological analog: alpha-band rhythm — gating of local processing windows; the strongest
and most-studied cortical oscillation effect on cognition.
What it would buy if included: transformers with causal attention struggle most at the
clause/phrase boundary — the 5–10 token window where syntactic structure closes and
opens. Attention can reach back 7 tokens, but it competes with all other tokens for attention
weight. A frozen 7-token compressed buffer is always present, zero competition cost. This
is the timescale where grammatical agreement, verb-argument binding, and local
coreference live. Expected observable effect: measurable perplexity drop on grammatical
coherence evals, subject-verb agreement, and pronoun resolution within sentences.
Closest prior art: Block-Recurrent Transformer (Hutchins et al., NeurIPS 2022) — explicit
recurrent state updated every fixed block of tokens, the closest match. Compressive
Transformer (Rae et al., ICLR 2020) — explicitly compresses past activations into fixed-size
memory. Memorizing Transformers (Wu et al., ICLR 2022). Multi-rate variants are
anticipated by Clockwork RNN (Koutník et al., ICML 2014) — modules update at different
fixed rates.
v1 rationale: The buffer is a known mechanism with strong prior art. Adding it to v1 would
confound the toroidal-sheet ablation — gains could be attributed to the buffer rather than
the sheet — and would add an expensive ablation slot to demonstrate decomposition.
Reconsider when: v2 has cleared its primary novelty claim and the multi-frequency /
cortical-rhythm framing becomes a paper subject in its own right, or v1 results show clause-
level coherence (subject-verb agreement, local pronoun resolution) underperforming
matched-FLOP baselines.
