11.11 Two-Level Toroidal Hierarchy (1024-dim post-loop top-k sheet)
Status: Deferred to v2 — candidate for its own paper.
Concept: A second toroidal sheet at the 1024-dim level, placed after the recurrent loop,
with top-k routing rather than always-on. Geometry: 32×32 grid, 9×9 patches with 1-cell
overlap, stride 8, 16 experts in a 4×4 expert grid. Each of the 16 macrocolumn experts is the
geometric integration point for exactly 4 of the 64 minicolumn experts on the level-1 (5184-
dim) sheet — the 4:1 spatial compression ratio is exact. With k=4 active experts per token
(recommended starting point), 25% of the upper sheet’s output is selectively integrated per
token; the router learns which spatial regions of the toroidal representation matter for a
given token’s final output.
What it would buy if included:
1. Content-adaptive final transformation — the toroidal sheet inside the loop applies the
same transformation structure to every token regardless of content. The top-k layer is
the first point in the architecture where a token’s content explicitly determines which
transformation is applied. Function words and rare technical terms can route to
differently specialized experts.
2. Hierarchical spatial compression with specialization — each top-k expert has
learned to compress and transform a specific region of the 5184-dim representational
space into the 1024-dim residual stream. The router learns which regions of that space
matter for a given token’s final representation — learned dimensional routing, not just
token routing.
3. Sparsity as implicit regularization — k < 16 forces each expert to specialize or become
redundant and stop being selected. Switch Transformer / GLaM findings: sparse expert
layers at the output stage tend to improve sample efficiency.
4. Decoupled loop and output optimization — without this layer, the loop must
simultaneously produce a good recurrent state for further iterations AND a good final
representation for the coda. The top-k layer separates these concerns: loop optimizes
for good recurrent state; top-k handles output preparation.
5. Soft ensemble of converged representations — k different spatial compressions
integrated per token gives ensemble-like robustness without the ensemble cost.
Effective receptive field calculation: an upper expert directly sees 100 of 5184 dims
(1.9%). A lower expert directly receives 4 × 100 = 400 dims (7.7%); via shared borders and
neighboring lower experts compressing 4 upper experts each, indirect receptive field
reaches ~1600 of 5184 (~31%). The lower sheet expands effective receptive field from ~2%
to ~31% in one compression step — genuine global integration emerging from local spatial
structure.
Why post-loop, not inside loop: inside loop pays top-k cost × R iterations (prohibitive on
RTX 3090); router instability interacts with recurrent dynamics; shared border dim
communication breaks if adjacent experts don’t both fire. Post-loop runs once (cheap), sees
fully converged representation (stable routing), and gives clean separation of
responsibilities.
Does it conflict with coda MLA? No — orthogonal mechanisms. Top-k MoE: per-token
content-adaptive FFN transformation (no cross-token mixing). Coda MLA: cross-token self-
attention (no content-adaptive routing). Same division of labor as FFN + attention in
standard transformer blocks. One interaction to monitor: tokens that should attend to each
other in coda MLA may route to different top-k experts, creating representational “dialects.”
Mitigation: routing-consistency auxiliary loss penalizing adjacent tokens routing to
completely disjoint expert sets.
Closest prior art: Hierarchical MoE (Riquelme et al.) — two-level expert hierarchy but
routing-based, not position-fixed, not spatial. Feature Pyramid Networks (Lin et al., 2017) —
multi-scale spatial hierarchy but vision only, no expert structure. U-Net (Ronneberger et al.,
2015) — encoder compression + skip, but vision, no MoE, no toroidal structure. Perceiver
(Jaegle et al., 2021) — high-to-low compression but via attention, not spatial. Switch
Transformer — single level, routing-based, no spatial structure. The geometrically-
consistent two-level toroidal hierarchy with 4:1 spatial compression has no direct prior art.
v1 rationale: this is a distinct novelty claim by itself. Folding it into v1 produces a paper with
two simultaneous novelty claims; reviewers cannot cleanly attribute gains between the
level-1 toroidal sheet (the v1 claim) and the level-2 hierarchy. It also strains the v1 ablation
budget: a “RTCC + standard top-k MoE post-loop” baseline becomes necessary to isolate
the toroidal-hierarchy contribution beyond standard MoE.
Reconsider when: v1 has landed and v2 is being scoped — at that point the two-level
hierarchy can be the v2 central claim, with v1’s toroidal sheet as the validated foundation.
The geometry extends cleanly to a third level (256-dim, 2×2=4 experts, hypercolumns) that
maps onto the known three-level cortical hierarchy (minicolumn → macrocolumn →
hypercolumn); that is v3 territory.
11.12 Phase Coupling Gate (cross-frequency modulation)
Status: Deferred to v2 or later.
Concept: Use a slow state (HSC_
mid or HSC
_slow) to gate the amplitude of a fast signal
(LIE). Implementation: lie_scale = sigmoid(gate_proj(HSC_mid)) ; then h = h + LIE(r) ×
lie_scale . Slow context modulates how strongly the depth signal is expressed at each
loop. Biological analog: cross-frequency phase-amplitude coupling — gamma amplitude
modulated by theta phase — the most sophisticated and best-studied feature of cortical
oscillation interaction.
What it would buy if included: very few parameters added, potentially high expressivity
gain. Lets the network learn that depth-signal strength should depend on global context
state (e.g., late in a long document the slow band is rich and depth signal is amplified; early
in a short prompt the depth signal is dampened to avoid overprocessing).
v1 rationale: highest-fidelity biological mechanism evaluated, but also the highest stability
risk. It is parasitic on the HSC bands (themselves deferred), and the cross-frequency
interaction can create training instabilities that are hard to diagnose at small scale.
Reconsider when: HSC bands are stable in v2 and the multi-frequency framing has
produced clean ablation results. Phase coupling becomes the natural “completing the
cortical analogy” addition for v3 or a follow-up paper.
11.13 Aggressive Overlap (≥4 cells)
Status: Deferred to v2.
Concept: Push the toroidal sheet’s overlap beyond v1’s sweep range. At overlap=4 with
10×10 patches, private core = 2×2 = 4 dims (4% private), approaching the biological 1–5%
intra-minicolumn estimate. This tests the extreme low-private-fraction regime motivated by
the cortical column literature, where the minicolumn is better understood as a
developmental and functional unit (shared tuning, common input) than as a synaptic
isolation unit.
What it would buy if included: direct test of the biology-dominant hypothesis against the
superposition-theory argument. Biology can afford low private % because neurons fire
sparsely (~95% silent at any moment, low interference in shared dims). RTCC’s experts are
always-active, which makes shared-dim interference structurally worse. If overlap=4 still
wins, it suggests the toroidal sheet’s representational capacity is dominated by lateral
communication rather than per-expert specialization — a strong biological-fidelity result.
v1 rationale: v1’s overlap sweep covers {0, 1, 2, 3} cells, spanning private fractions {100%,
64%, 36%, 16%} — the ML-theory consensus zone (conv overlap, ResNeXt grouped conv,
bottleneck minimum). Overlap=4 is a separate hypothesis (biology-dominant, against the
superposition argument). Adding it inflates the v1 ablation budget to test a hypothesis that
ML theory alone considers unlikely to win.
Reconsider when: v1 results show overlap=3 outperforming overlap=2 (the trend continues
into the biologically-faithful regime), or v2 adds a sparsity mechanism to the level-1 toroidal
sheet that resolves the always-on superposition concern.
12. Summary
RTCC v1 is the minimum architecture that supports the central paper claim — the Toroidal
Expert Sheet, used as the FFN replacement inside the recurrent core of an RDT, beats
matched-FLOP alternatives. Every architectural element in v1 is either part of the novel
contribution (toroidal sheet, cortical column analogy, expanded sheet projection with gated
residual, symmetric square grid, RDT integration, sink tokens), structurally necessary for the
architecture to function (LIE for loop symmetry breaking, LTI for stability, MLA for KV-cache
tractability across R loops), or a well-validated, low-cost engineering choice (hybrid Muon +
AdamW, GQA in prelude, FA2 throughout, factored embeddings, depth-scaled init).
Two prelude sizings were upsized from initial v1 draft values based on the leverage
argument that e is re-injected R=16 times into the recurrent core: input embedding
bottleneck 256 → 512 (cost +8.4M real params) and prelude FFN expansion 4× → 6× (cost
+37.7M real params). Both upgrades land in single-pass components where the cost is
upfront but the benefit compounds R-fold via e re-injection. Coda FFN is held at 4× because
it does not benefit from this leverage — it runs once at the end with no re-injection
multiplier.
Five elements that appeared in RTCC2 Revision 3 are deferred to v2 with explicit
reconsideration conditions: SSM, mHC, depth-wise per-loop LoRA, DSA, and 16K-context
training. Each was excluded for a specific reason: confounded ablation attribution (SSM),
optimizer complexity disproportionate to scale (mHC), structural gradient asymmetry under
truncated BPTT (depth-wise LoRA), or scope entanglement with long-context training that
itself is deferred (DSA, 16K).
Six additional elements emerged from the cortical-column / frequency-mechanism analysis
and are documented in Sections 11.8–11.13: TIE, Buffer_alpha, the HSC band family, the
two-level toroidal hierarchy with 4:1 spatial compression, phase coupling, and aggressive
overlap (≥4 cells). Each has the same scope-discipline rationale: confounded attribution,
prior-art mechanism that doesn’t carry the paper, or a novelty claim of sufficient size to
deserve its own paper.
The result is a 202M-real / 457M-effective architecture, a ~15B-token training run
completing in ~3.5 days on a single RTX 3090, and an ablation matrix of seven baselines
completing in ~25 additional days — a total v1 paper budget of roughly four weeks of GPU
time. The paper claim is sharp and the evidence is clean.
